INT. LIGHTHOUSE LENS ROOM - NIGHT (1887)

Brass. Prisms. A hundred glass faces catching one flame.

HOLLIS BRANT, 60s, salt-stiff beard, oils the turning gear. Slow. Careful.

SILAS, 15, his apprentice, polishes a prism with a rag. Too fast.

HOLLIS
Gently, boy.

SILAS
Yes, sir.

Wind howls. The tower groans.

A crack. Sharp. Glass.

Silas freezes. A hairline fracture crawls across the prism.

HOLLIS
Don't breathe.

SILAS
It was already -- I swear it --

Hollis crosses. Fast for his age. Grips Silas's wrist.

HOLLIS
How long have you known?

SILAS
Since Tuesday.

Hollis lifts the prism. Turns it to the flame. The crack is a bright wire.

HOLLIS
Tuesday.

Silence. The great lens turns. Light sweeps the sea.

The door bangs open. Rain blows in.

INSPECTOR AGNES FAIRWEATHER, 40s, black oilskin, ledger under one arm, stands dripping on the threshold.

AGNES
Evening, Mr. Brant. The Board sent me to check your lens.

Beat. Hollis sets the prism down on the cracked side. Hides it with his sleeve.

HOLLIS
Flawless, Inspector.

Agnes studies the prism. Studies Silas. Studies Hollis.

She sets down her ledger. Closes the door against the storm.

AGNES
Then you will not mind if I hold it.

Hollis exhales. Slow.

HOLLIS
Mind? Madam, I would be honoured.

He hands her the prism. Cracked side down.

She turns it over. Looks. Looks longer.

AGNES
Mm.

She wraps it in her scarf.

AGNES (CONT'D)
I'll say it caught the weather. Happens every winter.

Silas sits down hard on an oil drum.

AGNES (CONT'D)
Light the second flame, Mr. Brant. The fog is coming in.
